    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Yes, it merits progression based on hERG alone: the nominal margin is:\n\n`30 \u00b5M / 0.05 \u00b5M = 600-fold` over free clinical exposure.\n\nThat is reassuring, but hERG IC50 should not be treated as a standalone go/no-go criterion. Confirm that the assay and exposure estimates are comparable and account for active metabolites, peak concentrations, and uncertainty in protein binding.\n\n**Next tests:**\n\n1. Repeat hERG concentration-response testing under GLP-like conditions, including assay controls, Hill slope, and use/state dependence.\n2. Confirm free `Cmax` and assess unbound exposure at peak and projected supratherapeutic doses.\n3. Test major circulating metabolites for hERG activity.\n4. Run a broader cardiac ion-channel panel, especially Nav1.5 and Cav1.2.\n5. Evaluate action-potential duration in human iPSC-cardiomyocytes or Purkinje fibers.\n6. Conduct an in vivo cardiovascular/QT study at therapeutic and supratherapeutic exposures.\n\nI would prioritize it for the next study unless other liabilities exist, while using the integrated cardiac safety profile rather than the 600-fold margin alone.",
